WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.831
Welcome to Week Three of CS104: Method of Proof, Part One.

2
00:00:05.831 --> 00:00:11.662
We are moving from analysing logical forms to constructing mathematical arguments.

3
00:00:11.662 --> 00:00:18.024
A proof is a sequence of justified statements that explains why a

4
00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:20.144
claim must be true.

5
00:00:20.144 --> 00:00:26.506
Today we will study direct proof, counterexamples, proof by contrapositive, and proof

6
00:00:26.506 --> 00:00:27.566
by contradiction.

7
00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:33.927
We will also use congruence and the irrationality of square root of

8
00:00:33.927 --> 00:00:36.048
two as substantial examples.

9
00:00:36.048 --> 00:00:41.349
As you listen, watch the first move of each method.

10
00:00:41.349 --> 00:00:47.710
Choosing the right opening assumption is often the decision that makes the

11
00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:50.361
rest of a proof possible.

12
00:00:50.361 --> 00:00:52.709
Here is our route.

13
00:00:52.709 --> 00:00:59.754
Section three point one introduces the language surrounding proofs: definitions, propositions, axioms,

14
00:00:59.754 --> 00:01:02.102
direct arguments, and counterexamples.

15
00:01:02.102 --> 00:01:09.147
Section three point two changes the direction of an implication through the

16
00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:13.843
contrapositive and uses logical equivalences to reshape conclusions.

17
00:01:13.843 --> 00:01:20.888
Section three point three assumes the failed case and derives a contradiction.

18
00:01:20.888 --> 00:01:24.997
Along the way, keep asking four questions.

19
00:01:24.997 --> 00:01:27.346
What is the domain?

20
00:01:27.346 --> 00:01:32.042
Which definition turns the hypothesis into usable algebra?

21
00:01:32.042 --> 00:01:36.152
What exact form must the conclusion have?

22
00:01:36.152 --> 00:01:42.609
And, if we use contradiction, which two statements are finally incompatible?

23
00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:46.395
We begin with direct proofs and counterexamples.

24
00:01:46.395 --> 00:01:52.884
A direct proof establishes a conditional by moving from its hypothesis to

25
00:01:52.884 --> 00:01:53.966
its conclusion.

26
00:01:53.966 --> 00:02:00.455
A counterexample has the opposite purpose: it disproves a universal claim by

27
00:02:00.455 --> 00:02:05.322
locating one permitted case where the claimed property fails.

28
00:02:05.322 --> 00:02:08.026
Both methods rely on precision.

29
00:02:08.026 --> 00:02:11.811
A direct proof must justify every bridge.

30
00:02:11.811 --> 00:02:18.300
A counterexample must belong to the stated domain and must really make

31
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.463
the original statement false.

32
00:02:20.463 --> 00:02:24.447
Several kinds of mathematical statements play different roles.

33
00:02:24.447 --> 00:02:29.926
A proposition is a true mathematical statement supported by a proof.

34
00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:34.408
A particularly important proposition may be called a theorem.

35
00:02:34.408 --> 00:02:39.886
A lemma is a result proved mainly to support another theorem.

36
00:02:39.886 --> 00:02:42.376
Definitions and axioms are different.

37
00:02:42.376 --> 00:02:48.352
A definition fixes the precise meaning of a term; an axiom is

38
00:02:48.352 --> 00:02:52.834
accepted as a basic starting truth within the system.

39
00:02:52.834 --> 00:02:55.822
We do not prove a definition.

40
00:02:55.822 --> 00:02:57.316
We unpack it.

41
00:02:57.316 --> 00:03:03.293
And we do not repeatedly re-prove an axiom, but we must still

42
00:03:03.293 --> 00:03:06.779
respect its conditions when we use it.

43
00:03:06.779 --> 00:03:12.815
Proposition three point one point one says that if x is an

44
00:03:12.815 --> 00:03:17.342
even integer, then x squared is divisible by four.

45
00:03:17.342 --> 00:03:20.361
This is a universal conditional statement.

46
00:03:20.361 --> 00:03:26.397
To prove it, we need the definitions of even integers and divisibility,

47
00:03:26.397 --> 00:03:29.918
basic facts about integers, and algebraic manipulation.

48
00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:33.943
Notice how the conclusion determines the finish line.

49
00:03:33.943 --> 00:03:39.979
Saying four divides x squared means we must eventually express x squared

50
00:03:39.979 --> 00:03:42.494
as four times some integer.

51
00:03:42.494 --> 00:03:48.531
The hypothesis tells us where to start: express the even integer x

52
00:03:48.531 --> 00:03:51.046
as two times some integer.

53
00:03:51.046 --> 00:03:56.799
A mathematical definition gives a precise meaning that represents one object, property,

54
00:03:56.799 --> 00:03:57.758
or concept.

55
00:03:57.758 --> 00:04:03.511
An integer a is even if and only if there exists an

56
00:04:03.511 --> 00:04:07.347
integer n such that a equals two n.

57
00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:13.100
It is odd if and only if there exists an integer n

58
00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:16.935
such that a equals two n plus one.

59
00:04:16.935 --> 00:04:20.771
The phrase “there exists an integer” is essential.

60
00:04:20.771 --> 00:04:25.085
It introduces a witness whose integrality must be preserved.

61
00:04:25.085 --> 00:04:30.838
These definitions turn parity from a descriptive word into an equation that

62
00:04:30.838 --> 00:04:35.153
can be expanded, factored, and matched to another definition.

63
00:04:35.153 --> 00:04:40.587
For integers m and n, we say m divides n if and

64
00:04:40.587 --> 00:04:46.021
only if there exists an integer q such that n equals m

65
00:04:46.021 --> 00:04:46.474
q.

66
00:04:46.474 --> 00:04:50.549
Three divides twelve because twelve equals three times four.

67
00:04:50.549 --> 00:04:54.625
Seven divides twenty-one because twenty-one equals seven times three.

68
00:04:54.625 --> 00:05:00.059
If m does not divide n, we write a vertical bar with

69
00:05:00.059 --> 00:05:00.964
a slash.

70
00:05:00.964 --> 00:05:06.398
We call m a divisor or factor of n, and n a

71
00:05:06.398 --> 00:05:07.757
multiple of m.

72
00:05:07.757 --> 00:05:10.021
Again, the integer witness matters.

73
00:05:10.021 --> 00:05:15.455
To prove divisibility, exhibit the quotient as an integer rather than merely

74
00:05:15.455 --> 00:05:17.266
reporting a decimal calculation.

75
00:05:17.266 --> 00:05:20.422
Do we have to prove everything?

76
00:05:20.422 --> 00:05:20.948
No.

77
00:05:20.948 --> 00:05:23.578
A proof must start somewhere.

78
00:05:23.578 --> 00:05:29.889
Some statements have already been proved, and some basic properties are accepted

79
00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:31.993
without proof as axioms.

80
00:05:31.993 --> 00:05:35.149
Axioms do not make mathematics arbitrary.

81
00:05:35.149 --> 00:05:41.461
They declare the starting rules of the system so that later conclusions

82
00:05:41.461 --> 00:05:43.039
can be checked.

83
00:05:43.039 --> 00:05:49.351
When writing an elementary proof, familiar algebraic laws may remain in the

84
00:05:49.351 --> 00:05:55.136
background, but you must not use them outside their valid conditions.

85
00:05:55.136 --> 00:06:01.448
In particular, multiplication by an inverse requires that the number is nonzero.

86
00:06:01.448 --> 00:06:04.795
The real numbers satisfy familiar axioms.

87
00:06:04.795 --> 00:06:11.489
Identity laws say x plus zero equals x and x times one

88
00:06:11.489 --> 00:06:12.605
equals x.

89
00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:19.299
Inverse laws give an additive inverse for every real number and a

90
00:06:19.299 --> 00:06:23.204
multiplicative inverse for every nonzero real number.

91
00:06:23.204 --> 00:06:27.666
Commutative and associative laws allow reordering and regrouping.

92
00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:31.571
The distributive law connects multiplication with addition.

93
00:06:31.571 --> 00:06:37.707
Proofs often use these rules so naturally that they become invisible.

94
00:06:37.707 --> 00:06:44.401
Careful reasoning means recognising which rule licenses a step, especially when cancellation

95
00:06:44.401 --> 00:06:46.633
or division is involved.

96
00:06:46.633 --> 00:06:50.276
The integers are closed under addition and multiplication.

97
00:06:50.276 --> 00:06:55.741
If x and y are integers, then x plus y and x

98
00:06:55.741 --> 00:06:58.018
times y are also integers.

99
00:06:58.018 --> 00:07:03.483
These closure facts are accepted here as basic properties of the number

100
00:07:03.483 --> 00:07:03.939
system.

101
00:07:03.939 --> 00:07:09.404
Closure is often the quiet reason that a newly constructed quantity is

102
00:07:09.404 --> 00:07:11.225
a valid integer witness.

103
00:07:11.225 --> 00:07:16.690
If k is an integer, then k squared is an integer; if

104
00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:22.155
k and l are integers, then k plus l is an integer.

105
00:07:22.155 --> 00:07:27.620
A proof should make this clear whenever the conclusion depends on the

106
00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:29.897
witness staying inside the integers.

107
00:07:29.897 --> 00:07:35.280
A set S is closed under an operation when applying the operation

108
00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:40.662
to any members a and b of S always produces another member

109
00:07:40.662 --> 00:07:41.559
of S.

110
00:07:41.559 --> 00:07:46.941
The integers are not closed under division because one divided by two

111
00:07:46.941 --> 00:07:48.736
is not an integer.

112
00:07:48.736 --> 00:07:54.118
Even integers are closed under addition: two k plus two l equals

113
00:07:54.118 --> 00:07:56.361
two times k plus l.

114
00:07:56.361 --> 00:08:01.743
Odd integers are not closed under addition because the sum of two

115
00:08:01.743 --> 00:08:03.537
odd integers is even.

116
00:08:03.537 --> 00:08:08.919
Try the questions on the slide by translating the set property into

117
00:08:08.919 --> 00:08:09.368
algebra.

118
00:08:09.368 --> 00:08:14.750
Closure is a universal claim, so one permitted failure is enough to

119
00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:15.647
refute it.

120
00:08:15.647 --> 00:08:20.222
A direct proof establishes a conditional p implies q.

121
00:08:20.222 --> 00:08:26.321
It is a valid argument that starts with the hypothesis p and

122
00:08:26.321 --> 00:08:28.863
ends with the conclusion q.

123
00:08:28.863 --> 00:08:34.454
Between them, we use definitions, known properties, algebra, and earlier results.

124
00:08:34.454 --> 00:08:40.553
For a universal proposition, take an arbitrary object from the stated domain

125
00:08:40.553 --> 00:08:46.144
so that the reasoning does not depend on a special example.

126
00:08:46.144 --> 00:08:50.210
Do not assume the conclusion at the beginning.

127
00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:56.310
Instead, let its definition tell you the target form that your algebra

128
00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:57.835
must eventually produce.

129
00:08:57.835 --> 00:09:03.189
Proposition three point one point six says: if a is an odd

130
00:09:03.189 --> 00:09:06.312
number, then a plus one is even.

131
00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:09.436
Let a be an arbitrary odd integer.

132
00:09:09.436 --> 00:09:14.790
By definition, a equals two n plus one for some integer n.

133
00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:20.144
Adding one gives a plus one equals two n plus two, which

134
00:09:20.144 --> 00:09:23.268
factors as two times n plus one.

135
00:09:23.268 --> 00:09:28.622
Since n plus one is an integer, a plus one is twice

136
00:09:28.622 --> 00:09:29.514
an integer.

137
00:09:29.514 --> 00:09:33.976
This matches the definition of even, so the conclusion follows.

138
00:09:33.976 --> 00:09:39.330
The proof is short because the hypothesis immediately provides a useful algebraic

139
00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:39.777
representation.

140
00:09:39.777 --> 00:09:44.044
Now return to proposition three point one point one.

141
00:09:44.044 --> 00:09:46.889
Let x be an even integer.

142
00:09:46.889 --> 00:09:51.631
By definition, x equals two n for some integer n.

143
00:09:51.631 --> 00:09:55.425
Squaring gives x squared equals four n squared.

144
00:09:55.425 --> 00:10:00.641
Because n is an integer, n squared is also an integer.

145
00:10:00.641 --> 00:10:05.856
We have therefore written x squared as four times an integer.

146
00:10:05.856 --> 00:10:10.124
By the definition of divisibility, four divides x squared.

147
00:10:10.124 --> 00:10:15.814
Notice that the same equation performs two jobs: it carries out the

148
00:10:15.814 --> 00:10:20.556
algebra and presents the exact witness required by the conclusion.

149
00:10:20.556 --> 00:10:26.140
A universal statement claims there are no exceptions, so one counterexample is

150
00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.001
enough to disprove it.

151
00:10:28.001 --> 00:10:33.585
Consider: for all real a and b, if a squared equals b

152
00:10:33.585 --> 00:10:35.911
squared, then a equals b.

153
00:10:35.911 --> 00:10:41.030
Choose a equal to one and b equal to negative one.

154
00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:46.614
Both are real, and their squares are equal, so the hypothesis is

155
00:10:46.614 --> 00:10:47.079
true.

156
00:10:47.079 --> 00:10:52.197
But one is not negative one, so the conclusion is false.

157
00:10:52.197 --> 00:10:54.524
This is a genuine counterexample.

158
00:10:54.524 --> 00:11:00.108
An example where the hypothesis is false would not work, because a

159
00:11:00.108 --> 00:11:05.691
conditional is not defeated unless its hypothesis is true and its conclusion

160
00:11:05.691 --> 00:11:06.157
false.

161
00:11:06.157 --> 00:11:10.968
Section three point two introduces proof by contrapositive.

162
00:11:10.968 --> 00:11:18.184
This method is useful when the original hypothesis does not reveal a

163
00:11:18.184 --> 00:11:25.400
path to the conclusion, but negating the conclusion produces a helpful definition

164
00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.205
or algebraic form.

165
00:11:27.205 --> 00:11:34.421
Because an implication and its contrapositive are logically equivalent, proving the alternative

166
00:11:34.421 --> 00:11:39.232
direction establishes the original proposition without weakening it.

167
00:11:39.232 --> 00:11:44.839
Suppose we want to prove that if n squared is even, then

168
00:11:44.839 --> 00:11:46.241
n is even.

169
00:11:46.241 --> 00:11:50.914
A direct attempt starts with n squared equals two k.

170
00:11:50.914 --> 00:11:56.054
But that equation does not expose n as twice an integer.

171
00:11:56.054 --> 00:12:01.661
Taking a square root gives an expression whose integrality is unknown, so

172
00:12:01.661 --> 00:12:04.465
the intended witness does not appear.

173
00:12:04.465 --> 00:12:08.671
This does not show that the proposition is false.

174
00:12:08.671 --> 00:12:12.876
It tells us that the direct direction is unhelpful.

175
00:12:12.876 --> 00:12:18.484
We should change to an equivalent statement whose hypothesis gives us a

176
00:12:18.484 --> 00:12:20.353
usable representation of n.

177
00:12:20.353 --> 00:12:26.101
The contrapositive of “if p, then q” is “if not q, then

178
00:12:26.101 --> 00:12:31.850
not p.” We reverse the two parts and negate both, and the

179
00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.287
meaning is preserved.

180
00:12:33.287 --> 00:12:39.036
For the parity proposition, the original says: if n squared is even,

181
00:12:39.036 --> 00:12:40.952
then n is even.

182
00:12:40.952 --> 00:12:46.701
Its contrapositive says: if n is odd, then n squared is odd.

183
00:12:46.701 --> 00:12:52.450
This direction is promising because oddness immediately lets us write n as

184
00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:54.366
two k plus one.

185
00:12:54.366 --> 00:13:00.115
Do not confuse the contrapositive with the converse, which reverses without negating,

186
00:13:00.115 --> 00:13:03.468
or the inverse, which negates without reversing.

187
00:13:03.468 --> 00:13:07.782
We prove proposition three point two point one by contrapositive.

188
00:13:07.782 --> 00:13:12.959
Assume n is not even; because n is an integer, n must

189
00:13:12.959 --> 00:13:13.822
be odd.

190
00:13:13.822 --> 00:13:18.568
Write n equals two k plus one for some integer k.

191
00:13:18.568 --> 00:13:23.745
Expanding the square gives four k squared plus four k plus one,

192
00:13:23.745 --> 00:13:28.922
which is two times the integer two k squared plus two k,

193
00:13:28.922 --> 00:13:29.785
plus one.

194
00:13:29.785 --> 00:13:31.942
Therefore n squared is odd.

195
00:13:31.942 --> 00:13:37.119
We have proved not q implies not p, so the original proposition

196
00:13:37.119 --> 00:13:41.002
follows: whenever n squared is even, n is even.

197
00:13:41.002 --> 00:13:46.468
Proposition three point two point two says: if three does not divide

198
00:13:46.468 --> 00:13:51.478
the product m n, then three divides neither m nor n.

199
00:13:51.478 --> 00:13:56.944
Its contrapositive is easier: if three divides m or three divides n,

200
00:13:56.944 --> 00:13:59.221
then three divides m n.

201
00:13:59.221 --> 00:14:03.321
If three divides m, write m equals three k.

202
00:14:03.321 --> 00:14:08.787
Then m n equals three times k n, so three divides the

203
00:14:08.787 --> 00:14:09.242
product.

204
00:14:09.242 --> 00:14:12.886
If three divides n, the argument is symmetric.

205
00:14:12.886 --> 00:14:18.352
The logical form “p or q implies r” is equivalent to the

206
00:14:18.352 --> 00:14:23.818
conjunction of “p implies r” and “q implies r,” which justifies checking

207
00:14:23.818 --> 00:14:25.184
the two branches.

208
00:14:25.184 --> 00:14:30.553
Logical equivalence can also reshape a conclusion containing “or.” Proposition three point

209
00:14:30.553 --> 00:14:35.923
two point three says that if a b equals zero, then a

210
00:14:35.923 --> 00:14:38.607
equals zero or b equals zero.

211
00:14:38.607 --> 00:14:43.976
Let p mean a b equals zero, q mean a equals zero,

212
00:14:43.976 --> 00:14:46.661
and r mean b equals zero.

213
00:14:46.661 --> 00:14:52.030
The form p implies q or r is equivalent to p and

214
00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:53.820
not q implies r.

215
00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:59.189
In plain language: if the product is zero and a is not

216
00:14:59.189 --> 00:15:01.874
zero, then b must be zero.

217
00:15:01.874 --> 00:15:07.243
Assuming one alternative is unavailable gives us a focused conclusion to prove.

218
00:15:07.243 --> 00:15:12.682
Let a and b be real numbers with a b equal to

219
00:15:12.682 --> 00:15:15.855
zero and a not equal to zero.

220
00:15:15.855 --> 00:15:20.841
Since a is nonzero, its multiplicative inverse one over a exists.

221
00:15:20.841 --> 00:15:23.560
Multiply both sides by that inverse.

222
00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:28.999
Associativity lets us group one over a with a, the inverse law

223
00:15:28.999 --> 00:15:33.985
turns that product into one, and the identity law leaves b.

224
00:15:33.985 --> 00:15:36.251
The right side remains zero.

225
00:15:36.251 --> 00:15:38.064
Hence b equals zero.

226
00:15:38.064 --> 00:15:43.503
The assumption a not equal to zero is not incidental—it is exactly

227
00:15:43.503 --> 00:15:45.769
what makes the cancellation legitimate.

228
00:15:45.769 --> 00:15:50.353
Section three point three introduces proof by contradiction.

229
00:15:50.353 --> 00:15:57.229
Instead of aiming directly for the conclusion, we assume the situation that

230
00:15:57.229 --> 00:16:04.105
would make the proposition false and show that this situation cannot exist.

231
00:16:04.105 --> 00:16:10.980
A strong contradiction proof states the conflicting facts explicitly rather than ending

232
00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:14.991
with the vague phrase “this is impossible.”

233
00:16:14.991 --> 00:16:20.881
Integers a and b are congruent modulo a positive integer m when

234
00:16:20.881 --> 00:16:23.335
m divides a minus b.

235
00:16:23.335 --> 00:16:28.734
Equivalently, a equals b plus m k for some integer k.

236
00:16:28.734 --> 00:16:34.623
Twenty-four is congruent to ten modulo seven because their difference is fourteen.

237
00:16:34.623 --> 00:16:40.513
Negative two is congruent to eight modulo five because their difference is

238
00:16:40.513 --> 00:16:41.494
negative ten.

239
00:16:41.494 --> 00:16:47.384
The notation a is not congruent to b means the divisibility condition

240
00:16:47.384 --> 00:16:47.875
fails.

241
00:16:47.875 --> 00:16:53.764
Proposition three point three point two restates the definition in equation form,

242
00:16:53.764 --> 00:16:58.181
which is often the form needed inside a proof.

243
00:16:58.181 --> 00:17:00.603
Test the two statements carefully.

244
00:17:00.603 --> 00:17:06.415
The first is false: take a equal to two and b equal

245
00:17:06.415 --> 00:17:07.383
to three.

246
00:17:07.383 --> 00:17:13.195
Their product is six, so it is congruent to zero modulo six,

247
00:17:13.195 --> 00:17:17.554
but neither factor is congruent to zero modulo six.

248
00:17:17.554 --> 00:17:21.429
A composite modulus can be split across factors.

249
00:17:21.429 --> 00:17:27.241
The second statement is also false because the domain includes the positive

250
00:17:27.241 --> 00:17:29.662
integer n equal to one.

251
00:17:29.662 --> 00:17:35.474
Modulo one, every pair of integers is congruent, so one is congruent

252
00:17:35.474 --> 00:17:36.443
to zero.

253
00:17:36.443 --> 00:17:41.286
Boundary cases and composite structures are common sources of counterexamples.

254
00:17:41.286 --> 00:17:46.331
For a fixed modulus, congruence is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.

255
00:17:46.331 --> 00:17:52.384
It is reflexive because a minus a is zero, which every positive

256
00:17:52.384 --> 00:17:53.393
modulus divides.

257
00:17:53.393 --> 00:17:59.447
It is symmetric because divisibility of a minus b also gives divisibility

258
00:17:59.447 --> 00:18:02.473
of its negative, b minus a.

259
00:18:02.473 --> 00:18:08.527
It is transitive because adding the divisible differences a minus b and

260
00:18:08.527 --> 00:18:12.058
b minus c gives a minus c.

261
00:18:12.058 --> 00:18:18.112
These properties make congruence behave like equality within a fixed modulus, but

262
00:18:18.112 --> 00:18:22.652
the modulus must remain the same throughout the argument.

263
00:18:22.652 --> 00:18:28.585
To prove a universal conditional p of x implies q of x

264
00:18:28.585 --> 00:18:34.519
by contradiction, assume there is an x for which p is true

265
00:18:34.519 --> 00:18:36.496
and q is false.

266
00:18:36.496 --> 00:18:40.452
This is the negation of the universal implication.

267
00:18:40.452 --> 00:18:46.385
Then use both assumptions, together with definitions and known facts, until an

268
00:18:46.385 --> 00:18:47.374
impossibility appears.

269
00:18:47.374 --> 00:18:53.307
The contradiction may be p and not p, an integer being both

270
00:18:53.307 --> 00:18:58.252
even and odd, or a violation of an established condition.

271
00:18:58.252 --> 00:19:04.185
Once the failed case is impossible, the original universal statement must be

272
00:19:04.185 --> 00:19:04.679
true.

273
00:19:04.679 --> 00:19:09.940
Proposition three point three point four says there is no integer n

274
00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:14.325
such that two n is congruent to one modulo four.

275
00:19:14.325 --> 00:19:17.832
Suppose, for contradiction, that such an n exists.

276
00:19:17.832 --> 00:19:23.093
By the definition of congruence, two n equals one plus four k

277
00:19:23.093 --> 00:19:24.847
for some integer k.

278
00:19:24.847 --> 00:19:27.039
The left side is even.

279
00:19:27.039 --> 00:19:32.300
The right side is odd because four k is even and adding

280
00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:34.054
one makes it odd.

281
00:19:34.054 --> 00:19:37.561
An integer cannot be both even and odd.

282
00:19:37.561 --> 00:19:42.384
That parity conflict is the contradiction, so no such n exists.

283
00:19:42.384 --> 00:19:47.755
Let x and y be positive real numbers with x not equal

284
00:19:47.755 --> 00:19:48.650
to y.

285
00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:54.021
We prove x over y plus y over x is greater than

286
00:19:54.021 --> 00:19:54.468
two.

287
00:19:54.468 --> 00:19:58.497
Assume instead that the sum is at most two.

288
00:19:58.497 --> 00:20:03.867
Since x y is positive, multiplying by it preserves the inequality and

289
00:20:03.867 --> 00:20:08.791
gives x squared plus y squared at most two x y.

290
00:20:08.791 --> 00:20:14.162
Rearranging gives open parenthesis x minus y close parenthesis squared at most

291
00:20:14.162 --> 00:20:14.609
zero.

292
00:20:14.609 --> 00:20:19.085
A real square is nonnegative, so it must equal zero.

293
00:20:19.085 --> 00:20:22.218
Then x equals y, contradicting the hypothesis.

294
00:20:22.218 --> 00:20:26.246
Positivity justified both the fractions and the inequality step.

295
00:20:26.246 --> 00:20:31.891
We now prepare the classic proof that square root of two is

296
00:20:31.891 --> 00:20:32.362
irrational.

297
00:20:32.362 --> 00:20:37.537
An irrational number is a real number that is not rational.

298
00:20:37.537 --> 00:20:43.182
A rational number can be written as a quotient m over n

299
00:20:43.182 --> 00:20:45.535
of integers, with n positive.

300
00:20:45.535 --> 00:20:51.180
Equivalent fractions may represent the same rational value, so we will need

301
00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:53.532
a disciplined choice of representation.

302
00:20:53.532 --> 00:20:59.178
The contradiction will not be that a quotient exists; it will be

303
00:20:59.178 --> 00:21:04.823
that a quotient chosen in lowest terms is forced to have a

304
00:21:04.823 --> 00:21:05.764
common factor.

305
00:21:05.764 --> 00:21:11.350
An integer d is a common factor, or common divisor, of integers

306
00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:14.609
a and b when d divides both.

307
00:21:14.609 --> 00:21:18.798
Three is a common factor of nine and eighteen.

308
00:21:18.798 --> 00:21:23.919
Their common factors include plus or minus one, three, and nine.

309
00:21:23.919 --> 00:21:29.505
By contrast, eight and fifteen have no positive common factor greater than

310
00:21:29.505 --> 00:21:29.971
one.

311
00:21:29.971 --> 00:21:35.557
Common factors let us describe when a fraction can be reduced, and

312
00:21:35.557 --> 00:21:41.143
that idea becomes the invariant at the heart of the square-root proof.

313
00:21:41.143 --> 00:21:45.399
Every rational number has more than one quotient representation.

314
00:21:45.399 --> 00:21:51.073
Two thirds, four sixths, and six ninths all represent the same value.

315
00:21:51.073 --> 00:21:56.746
We say m over n is in lowest terms when m and

316
00:21:56.746 --> 00:22:00.529
n have no common factor greater than one.

317
00:22:00.529 --> 00:22:05.257
Choosing lowest terms is always possible for a rational number.

318
00:22:05.257 --> 00:22:10.458
This condition gives us something definite that later algebra cannot violate.

319
00:22:10.458 --> 00:22:16.132
If the algebra forces both m and n to be even, it

320
00:22:16.132 --> 00:22:19.915
will contradict the way the fraction was chosen.

321
00:22:19.915 --> 00:22:22.722
What is square root of two?

322
00:22:22.722 --> 00:22:28.336
It is the positive real number r such that r squared equals

323
00:22:28.336 --> 00:22:28.804
two.

324
00:22:28.804 --> 00:22:34.419
Saying that square root of two is irrational is therefore equivalent to

325
00:22:34.419 --> 00:22:40.033
the conditional statement: if r is a positive real number with r

326
00:22:40.033 --> 00:22:43.776
squared equal to two, then r is irrational.

327
00:22:43.776 --> 00:22:49.391
Writing the claim in this form separates the existence and defining equation

328
00:22:49.391 --> 00:22:53.134
of r from the property we must prove.

329
00:22:53.134 --> 00:22:58.281
We will keep r squared equals two and temporarily deny irrationality.

330
00:22:58.281 --> 00:23:02.917
Proposition three point three point eight states the claim formally.

331
00:23:02.917 --> 00:23:08.480
Let p of r mean r squared equals two, and let q

332
00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.261
of r mean r is irrational.

333
00:23:11.261 --> 00:23:16.824
To prove the implication by contradiction, assume there exists a positive real

334
00:23:16.824 --> 00:23:21.460
r for which p is true and q is false.

335
00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:27.024
In plain language, assume r squared equals two and r is rational.

336
00:23:27.024 --> 00:23:32.587
The rationality assumption lets us write r as an integer quotient in

337
00:23:32.587 --> 00:23:37.223
lowest terms, while the square equation will constrain that quotient.

338
00:23:37.223 --> 00:23:42.291
Write r as m over n in lowest terms, so m and

339
00:23:42.291 --> 00:23:45.670
n have no common factor greater than one.

340
00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:50.738
From r squared equals two, we get m squared equals two n

341
00:23:50.738 --> 00:23:51.161
squared.

342
00:23:51.161 --> 00:23:56.229
Thus m squared is even, and proposition three point two point one

343
00:23:56.229 --> 00:23:58.341
tells us m is even.

344
00:23:58.341 --> 00:24:00.453
Write m equals two k.

345
00:24:00.453 --> 00:24:05.521
Substitution gives four k squared equals two n squared, hence n squared

346
00:24:05.521 --> 00:24:07.211
equals two k squared.

347
00:24:07.211 --> 00:24:12.279
So n squared is even, and the same proposition tells us n

348
00:24:12.279 --> 00:24:13.124
is even.

349
00:24:13.124 --> 00:24:16.080
Therefore two divides both m and n.

350
00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:21.149
This contradicts the assumption that m over n was in lowest terms.

351
00:24:21.149 --> 00:24:25.024
The proof structure is worth reading carefully.

352
00:24:25.024 --> 00:24:31.668
We assumed both r squared equals two and a lowest-terms rational representation

353
00:24:31.668 --> 00:24:34.436
r equals m over n.

354
00:24:34.436 --> 00:24:41.080
The algebra forced the conclusion that m and n have common factor

355
00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:41.634
two.

356
00:24:41.634 --> 00:24:44.956
The contradiction concerns the lowest-terms condition.

357
00:24:44.956 --> 00:24:51.600
We are not claiming that a quotient with a common factor is

358
00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:54.922
irrational—four sixths is rational but reducible.

359
00:24:54.922 --> 00:25:01.566
Because every rational number has a lowest-terms representation, no alternative fraction can

360
00:25:01.566 --> 00:25:03.780
rescue the rationality assumption.

361
00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:07.656
Therefore square root of two is irrational.

362
00:25:07.656 --> 00:25:13.033
Contrapositive and contradiction are related but give us different working targets.

363
00:25:13.033 --> 00:25:18.899
For a contrapositive proof, assume not q and try to prove not

364
00:25:18.899 --> 00:25:19.388
p.

365
00:25:19.388 --> 00:25:25.254
For a contradiction proof of p implies q, assume both p and

366
00:25:25.254 --> 00:25:28.187
not q, then derive an impossibility.

367
00:25:28.187 --> 00:25:34.053
A contrapositive proof can be viewed as a contradiction proof if p

368
00:25:34.053 --> 00:25:39.430
is also present: deriving not p creates p and not p.

369
00:25:39.430 --> 00:25:45.296
In practice, choose the method whose assumptions reveal the most useful definitions

370
00:25:45.296 --> 00:25:50.185
and algebra, and state clearly which form you are proving.

371
00:25:50.185 --> 00:25:56.333
Finish with proposition three point three point nine: the sum of any

372
00:25:56.333 --> 00:26:00.432
rational number and any irrational number is irrational.

373
00:26:00.432 --> 00:26:04.531
Let r be rational and s be irrational.

374
00:26:04.531 --> 00:26:09.142
Suppose, for contradiction, that r plus s is rational.

375
00:26:09.142 --> 00:26:15.291
Rational numbers are closed under subtraction, so subtracting the rational number r

376
00:26:15.291 --> 00:26:20.927
from the rational number r plus s produces another rational number.

377
00:26:20.927 --> 00:26:23.489
But that difference is s.

378
00:26:23.489 --> 00:26:29.637
We have derived that s is rational, contradicting the hypothesis that s

379
00:26:29.637 --> 00:26:30.662
is irrational.

380
00:26:30.662 --> 00:26:33.736
Therefore r plus s is irrational.

381
00:26:33.736 --> 00:26:38.860
Try to write this proof yourself before checking the reasoning.

382
00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:42.448
Let us bring the methods together.

383
00:26:42.448 --> 00:26:49.027
Use direct proof when the hypothesis already gives a useful representation.

384
00:26:49.027 --> 00:26:56.203
Use a counterexample to disprove a universal claim by making its hypothesis

385
00:26:56.203 --> 00:26:58.595
true and conclusion false.

386
00:26:58.595 --> 00:27:05.772
Use contraposition when negating the conclusion exposes a definition you can manipulate.

387
00:27:05.772 --> 00:27:12.948
Use contradiction when the original hypothesis and the negated conclusion create constraints

388
00:27:12.948 --> 00:27:14.743
that cannot coexist.

389
00:27:14.743 --> 00:27:21.919
Throughout, define the domain, preserve integer witnesses, justify inverses and inequality operations,

390
00:27:21.919 --> 00:27:24.909
and name the exact contradiction.

391
00:27:24.909 --> 00:27:32.086
Complete the Week Three mastery check now, then explain aloud why each

392
00:27:32.086 --> 00:27:35.520
worked proposition used its chosen method.
